CMVM / School of Regeneration and Repair, Centre for Inflammation Research

Full-time: 35 hours per week

Fixed-term: for 4 years, with an opportunity for mutually agreed upon extension

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Where discovery never stops. Be part of something bigger.

Roles for every stage of your career.

We offer careers and roles in just about every area you can think of. Whether you are looking for entry-level, mid-career or roles at the top of your chosen specialism, we can offer you a career where you can flourish and make a real difference in the communities around us while contributing to the world at large. Our people come from a wide range of backgrounds, each bringing their abilities and experiences to our organisation. ​

The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We look for the best in the field across all disciplines and provide a working environment where academics can develop their careers and passion for their chosen subject area. We offer the full range of academic roles and have a genuine focus on our students’ performance and wellbeing.

&nbsp;

The Opportunity:

We are looking for a motivated and enthusiastic Research Assistant to join the Clancy laboratory at the Centre for Inflammation Research. Supported by an eight-year Wellcome Trust Career Development Award, our research aims to understand how immune cell signalling is regulated at the molecular level.

As part of our research group, you will contribute to lab-based projects focusing on the structural and functional characterisation of proteins that regulate T cell and macrophage function. Our research combines of structural, molecular, and cell biology techniques, with in vivo models to address fundamental questions in immunology.&nbsp;

You’ll work closely with the Principal Investigator and other lab members to contribute to a long-term research programme. You will contribute to day-to-day research activities of the lab and gain experience in a broad range of experimental techniques.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Your skills and attributes for success:&nbsp;

Degree in relevant discipline with relevant research experience or equivalent.

Demonstrable experience of research in the broad area of the related project.

Proven experience of running analysis, interpreting data using relevant analysis software and experience managing and maintaining genetically modified mouse colonies to support the research in the field of structural biology OR immunology OR cell biology with demonstratable experience

Ability to gather research data using defined methods

Technical proficiency and demonstratable expertise with molecular cloning, protein expression, and protein purification.

&nbsp;

This post is full-time (35 hours per week).

